# Getting started

= This file is a *template*. When you save your work, use Save As so this original stays available.

[[Notes like this are Fountain comments and do not appear in the PDF.]]

/* Boneyard / draft scratch — also omitted from export
   Use this for lines you are not ready to cut completely. */

INT. WRITER'S ROOM - DAY

A corkboard full of index cards. Soft laptop GLOW. Coffee steam rises.

THE WRITER sits, fingers hovering over the keys.

WRITER
(to self)
Start with a scene heading. Then action. Then people talking.

They type. The screen fills with words.

CUT TO:

EXT. CITY STREET - CONTINUOUS

Rain-slick asphalt. Neon reflections. Foot traffic moves past.

.A FORCED SCENE HEADING
(Scene headings can also be forced with a leading period.)

ACTION lines are normal paragraphs. They describe what we *see* and *hear*.

You can emphasise with *italics*, **bold**, or ***both*** in Fountain.

CHARACTER CUES are UPPERCASE on their own line after a blank line.

ALICE
Hello. This is dialogue.

BOB (V.O.)
(wry)
And this is a parenthetical under a character with a voice-over extension.

ALICE
We can go back and forth.

BOB
For as long as the scene needs.

>SMASH CUT TO:

INT. COFFEE SHOP - NIGHT

Same city, later. Warmer light. Soft MUSIC under.

# Dual dialogue example

= Place a caret ^ after the second character name for dual dialogue in full Fountain tools.

ALICE
I thought you'd never ask.

BOB ^
I was waiting for the right moment.

# Transitions and centred text

FADE TO:

>THE END<

# Markdown-style extras Fountain understands

## Sections use hash marks
### Nested outline levels help you organise

= Synopses start with an equals sign (single, not a page break).

Page breaks look like this:

===

INT. EPILOGUE - DAY

One last beat. Keep writing. Save often.

WRITER
(smiling)
And when you're done... Save As under a new name.
The starter template will still be here next time.

FADE OUT.
